      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "THE QUESTION",
      "estimated_duration_s": 8.0,
      "narration_text": "Two drugs. Same antibody. One treats HER2-low tumors. The other cannot. Here's the question we need to answer: if the antibody is identical, why does it matter?",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "hold",
        "form": "text-card",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "FormACard",
          "provenance": "proven-core/FormACard",
          "version": "1",
          "props": {
            "lines": [
              "Two drugs. Same antibody.",
              "One treats HER2-low. The other cannot.",
              "If the antibody is identical — why?"
            ],
            "dark": false
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B03.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 10.45,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-30T17:27:34"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "THE PROBLEM",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10.0,
      "narration_text": "The antibody controls exactly one step: which cell gets bound. In a HER2-low tumor, only a few cells carry the antigen. Most do not. The antibody reaches only those few.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "reveal",
        "form": "text-card",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "FormACard",
          "provenance": "proven-core/FormACard",
          "version": "1",
          "props": {
            "lines": [
              "The antibody chooses one step:",
              "which cell gets bound.",
              "HER2-low tumor · most cells never bind"
            ],
            "dark": false
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B04.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 11.97,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-30T17:27:34"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "act": "THE PROBLEM",
      "estimated_duration_s": 9.0,
      "narration_text": "The intuitive guess: both drugs underperform equally. Antigen is scarce, so little binds, so little kills. Pick whichever tolerates better. That guess turns out to be wrong — and the reason is in what happens after the antibody binds.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "highlight",
        "form": "text-card",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "FormACard",
          "provenance": "proven-core/FormACard",
          "version": "1",
          "props": {
            "lines": [
              "The intuitive guess:",
              "both drugs underperform equally.",
              "Wrong — the split is downstream of binding."
            ],
            "dark": false
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B05.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 14.83,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-30T17:27:34"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "act": "THE MECHANISM",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11.0,
      "narration_text": "T-DM1 uses a non-cleavable linker. After the antibody binds and the drug enters the cell, the freed payload carries a charged fragment. Here's why that matters: charged means it cannot cross the cell membrane.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "reveal",
        "form": "text-card",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "FormACard",
          "provenance": "proven-core/FormACard",
          "version": "1",
          "props": {
            "lines": [
              "T-DM1 · non-cleavable linker",
              "freed payload carries a charged fragment",
              "charged = cannot cross the membrane"
            ],
            "dark": false
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B06.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 14.98,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-30T17:27:34"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "act": "THE MECHANISM",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10.0,
      "narration_text": "So T-DM1 kills only the cell it entered. The HER2-negative cells around it — the majority in a HER2-low tumor — survive untouched. The payload is trapped. The tumor largely isn't.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "hold",
        "form": "text-card",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "FormACard",
          "provenance": "proven-core/FormACard",
          "version": "1",
          "props": {
            "lines": [
              "T-DM1 kills only the cell it entered.",
              "HER2-negative neighbors survive untouched.",
              "Payload trapped · tumor largely isn't."
            ],
            "dark": false
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B07.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 13.27,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B07.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-30T17:27:34"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "act": "THE MECHANISM",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11.0,
      "narration_text": "T-DXd uses a cleavable linker, and its freed payload is membrane-permeable. Here's where it gets interesting: released inside one HER2-positive cell, it diffuses across the membrane into neighboring cells — including the ones that never expressed HER2.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "reveal",
        "form": "text-card",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "FormACard",
          "provenance": "proven-core/FormACard",
          "version": "1",
          "props": {
            "lines": [
              "T-DXd · cleavable linker",
              "freed payload is membrane-permeable",
              "diffuses to neighbors · even HER2-negative"
            ],
            "dark": false
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B08.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 16.17,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B08.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-30T17:27:34"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "act": "THE IMPLICATION",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10.0,
      "narration_text": "The antibody is not the whole mechanism. It finds the cell. What happens next depends entirely on whether the payload can cross a membrane. That single physical property — membrane permeability — is what separates these two drugs.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "highlight",
        "form": "text-card",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "FormACard",
          "provenance": "proven-core/FormACard",
          "version": "1",
          "props": {
            "lines": [
              "The antibody finds the cell.",
              "What happens next: can the payload cross?",
              "Membrane permeability separates them."
            ],
            "dark": false
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B09.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 14.95,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B09.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-30T17:27:34"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "act": "THE EXAMPLE",
      "estimated_duration_s": 14.0,
      "narration_text": "In a HER2-low tumor patch: five HER2-positive cells are all the antibody can reach. T-DM1 kills those five. T-DXd from the same five entry points spreads membrane-permeable payload to roughly forty surrounding cells. Same antibody. Eight times more kills. The bystander effect is the whole story.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "drawon",
        "form": "text-card",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "FormACard",
          "provenance": "proven-core/FormACard",
          "version": "1",
          "props": {
            "lines": [
              "HER2-low patch · 5 entry points",
              "T-DM1 kills 5 · T-DXd kills ~40",
              "same antibody · 8× more · bystander"
            ],
            "dark": false
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B10.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 19.52,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B10.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-30T17:27:34"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "act": "RECAP",
      "estimated_duration_s": 8.0,
      "narration_text": "Same antibody. One payload stays put — confined by charge. One payload spreads — carried by membrane permeability. That is why T-DXd, not T-DM1, treats HER2-low breast cancer. The antibody targets. The payload determines reach.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "hold",
        "form": "text-card",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "FormACard",
